{"assets":[],"contentKind":"statement","contestId":"1184","diagnostics":[],"document":{"children":[{"attrs":{"level":1,"role":"title"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"A2. Heidi Learns Hashing (Medium)"}],"kind":"heading"},{"attrs":{"role":"time_limit"},"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"time limit per test"}],"kind":"container"},{"kind":"text","text":"2 seconds"}],"kind":"container"},{"attrs":{"role":"memory_limit"},"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"memory limit per test"}],"kind":"container"},{"kind":"text","text":"256 megabytes"}],"kind":"container"},{"attrs":{"role":"body"},"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"After learning about polynomial hashing, Heidi decided to learn about shift-xor hashing. In particular, she came across this interesting problem."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Given a bitstring $$$y \\in \\{0,1\\}^n$$$ find out the number of different $$$k$$$ ($$$0 \\leq k \\lt n$$$) such that there exists $$$x \\in \\{0,1\\}^n$$$ for which $$$y = x \\oplus \\mbox{shift}^k(x).$$$"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"In the above, $$$\\oplus$$$ is the xor operation and $$$\\mbox{shift}^k$$$ is the operation of shifting a bitstring cyclically to the right $$$k$$$ times. For example, $$$001 \\oplus 111 = 110$$$ and $$$\\mbox{shift}^3(00010010111000) = 00000010010111$$$."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"section"},{"attrs":{"role":"input_specification"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":2},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Input"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"The first line contains an integer $$$n$$$ ($$$1 \\leq n \\leq 2 \\cdot 10^5$$$), the length of the bitstring $$$y$$$."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"The second line contains the bitstring $$$y$$$."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"section"},{"attrs":{"role":"output_specification"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":2},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Output"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Output a single integer: the number of suitable values of $$$k$$$."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"section"},{"attrs":{"role":"samples"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":2},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Example"}],"kind":"heading"},{"attrs":{"role":"sample"},"children":[{"attrs":{"role":"sample_input"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Input"}],"kind":"heading"},{"kind":"code_block","text":"\n4\n1010\n"}],"kind":"section"},{"attrs":{"role":"sample_output"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Output"}],"kind":"heading"},{"kind":"code_block","text":"\n3\n"}],"kind":"section"}],"kind":"section"}],"kind":"section"},{"attrs":{"role":"note"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":2},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Note"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"In the first example:"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"attrs":{"ordered":false},"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":" $$$1100\\oplus \\mbox{shift}^1(1100) = 1010$$$ "}],"kind":"list_item"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":" $$$1000\\oplus \\mbox{shift}^2(1000) = 1010$$$ "}],"kind":"list_item"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":" $$$0110\\oplus \\mbox{shift}^3(0110) = 1010$$$ "}],"kind":"list_item"}],"kind":"list"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"There is no $$$x$$$ such that $$$x \\oplus x = 1010$$$, hence the answer is $$$3$$$."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"section"}],"kind":"document"},"index":"A2","problemCode":"1184A2","schema":2,"sourceKind":"html","sourceUrl":"https://codeforces.com/contest/1184/problem/A2"}